WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.100
-I think most people thought
I peaked in my second year

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.934
in the league,
and I didn't peak at 21.

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:07.501
-Alley-oop for Brown.

00:00:07.501 --> 00:00:11.434
And it goes coast to coast.
-Once again, Brown.

00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:13.968
-During the draft process,

00:00:13.968 --> 00:00:16.334
an exec said
that you were too smart.

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:17.300
-That was true.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.968
-To Brown.

00:00:18.968 --> 00:00:21.000
-Knocks down a three!

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
-Was your role last year, and
what was your role this year,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.000
and what do you attribute
that leap to?

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.033
-Space. I think we had
a lot of guys last year.

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:28.334
I think that wasn't a secret.

00:00:28.334 --> 00:00:30.033
I was asked
to come off the bench.

00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:36.000
♪♪

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.634
-My name is Jaylen Brown --

00:00:37.634 --> 00:00:41.501
Cal Bear, Boston Celtic,
Georgia boy, Atlanta native,

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:43.234
and she about to be all up
in my business,

00:00:43.234 --> 00:00:46.167
but we're going to take it there
with Taylor Rooks.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.767
-Jaylen Brown.
That's a throw.

00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:51.567
Jaylen Brown knocks down
a huge shot.

00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:54.100
-What is this? What is this?

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.100
-Acne Studio.
Just came in today.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.901
-This is fine.
This is my vibe right here.

00:00:57.901 --> 00:01:00.667
-This is it,
with jeans and pants?

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:02.601
We got some sweatpants.
-You know me.

00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:06.167
-Yes, sir.

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:07.567
-Okay, so how are you
as a client?

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:09.834
Like, you know what you like.
Is it easy for you?

00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:11.100
-Yeah, we've been
working together for --

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:12.467
-A year-plus.

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:14.200
-She picks the clothes,
and then I'll put it together.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.901
-All right, now, Jaylen,
I've always wondered this,

00:01:15.901 --> 00:01:16.934
like, as I look
at these clothes.

00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:18.000
Now, be honest.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.501
How do you decide
what you're going to wear

00:01:19.501 --> 00:01:22.200
for, like, a regional game
or a national game,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.300
and is there a thought
process behind it?

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.734
For me, it depends.

00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:28.434
Like, how I dress
is just basically,

00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:29.667
like, how I'm feeling
at the time.

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:30.934
Like, I try to, like, mood it,

00:01:30.934 --> 00:01:32.033
like,
how I'm going to be feeling,

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:33.701
like, if we're playing
a certain team,

00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:35.434
Like, I'll have a fit
already put together,

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:37.567
but I'll only wear it
when I feel

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:38.934
I'm feeling that fit.
Does that make sense?

00:01:38.934 --> 00:01:40.334
-No, I feel you,
'cause, see, in my head,

00:01:40.334 --> 00:01:42.367
I thought it was like, "All
right, listen, we're playing

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:43.767
the -- I don't know,
we're playing the Lakers.

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:44.934
I got to get the fit up.

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:47.133
I got to get the pic
in the tunnel."

00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:48.534
You don't ever think
that at all.

00:01:48.534 --> 00:01:51.067
-I mean, everybody does.
I want to win the game first.

00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:52.267
-Absolutely.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:53.534
-That's a topic of discussion
right now.

00:01:53.534 --> 00:01:55.000
-Oh yes. Who was it?
J.J. Redick was talking about --

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.634
-J.J. Redick said that.
So I'm all about winning first.

00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:59.100
But for me, always being seen,
got to look good.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.567
So she helps me look good.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:03.100
-She don't got to do much.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.701
-Ah, here we go.

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:06.567
Yes, you do.
I know how you dress him.

00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:08.567
-[ Chuckles ]

00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:10.300
-You know, it's interesting,
'cause last year,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.200
when they announced
the extension,

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.501
a lot of people,
they're, like --

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:15.634
Twitter, almost felt like it
was too much,

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:17.100
they shouldn't have paid you.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.734
How did that feel
knowing that there was

00:02:18.734 --> 00:02:21.501
this kind of negative reaction
to the extension?

00:02:21.501 --> 00:02:22.934
-I kind of anticipated it.

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:25.033
I kind of felt like that was
what's going to be said.

00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:26.400
I think most people
thought I peaked

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.100
in my second year in the league,
and I didn't peak at 21.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.567
I'm only going to get
better from here.

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:34.834
Certain people think that
I'm playing out of my mind now

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:36.100
'cause they didn't expect it.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.434
You know, so that's part
of the reason why I cut my hair,

00:02:38.434 --> 00:02:42.033
'cause people will put you in
a box per certain stipulations

00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:44.400
or try to identify you
a certain way,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.133
and I wanted people to kind of
see me different this year.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.067
-I think a lot of people
wouldn't know how to bounce back

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:51.100
from maybe
what was a tough year.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.834
Mentally, how did you
become better after that?

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:54.901
-It was tough.

00:02:54.901 --> 00:02:59.267
I suffer from a lot of anxiety.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:00.968
When you have
some of these expectations,

00:03:00.968 --> 00:03:02.834
especially in a city
like Boston,

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:04.334
which is, like,
they want to win,

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:05.434
if they're not performing,

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:06.968
you start to lose
your confidence,

00:03:06.968 --> 00:03:08.000
you start to doubt yourself.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.400
A part of me
coming into this year

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.868
was, like, not dealing with
that negativity,

00:03:11.868 --> 00:03:14.100
like, saying,
"'F' that. Fuck that."

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.033
I said,
"Cut my hair, cut my care."

00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:20.000
Like, "Let him go,
stop caring what people think,"

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.634
and I reached a new pinnacle
of happiness and peace.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.033
Yes.

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:29.634
-Okay. He's feeling himself.

00:03:29.634 --> 00:03:31.200
Please take off the jacket.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.300
-No, I love it.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:35.334
-Please take it off.

00:03:35.334 --> 00:03:37.667
I see you, like,
attempting some waves.

00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:39.667
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:42.534
-Oh.
-Little bit.

00:03:42.534 --> 00:03:44.067
It's a little swimmy.

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:46.534
-Yo, she just tried
to play me like that.

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:48.033
-He's waving a little bit.

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:50.033
[ Laughter ]
-It's a process.

00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:51.400
You know what I mean?
Get the close-up.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.234
Hold up, man. Get the close-up.
You know what I mean?

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.200
Waves coming in.
You know what I mean?

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.868
360. All right back up.
That's it.

00:03:57.868 --> 00:04:01.334
-Yeah, see, you can't see it
when you're close.

00:04:01.334 --> 00:04:03.868
-She got jokes today, okay.
I'm letting you rock.

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:05.133
I ain't say nothing
about your ankles.

00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:06.567
Look. Zoom in.
-Ah!

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:07.834
They ashy?

00:04:07.834 --> 00:04:09.000
-They look -- look,
don't try to hide.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.767
-No, you can zoom in
at my ankles. Zoom in.

00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:14.934
It's fair. It's fair game.
Do you have any lotion for me?

00:04:14.934 --> 00:04:16.167
-Nope.
-No you don't. Okay.

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:17.234
Can we see your ankles?

00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:20.968
-Nope. Next fit.

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:25.701
-I saw a story when you were
playing the Jazz last season,

00:04:25.701 --> 00:04:27.901
and, in game,
Donovan Mitchell came up

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:29.400
and said something to you.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.801
-Mm-hmm.
-Can you tell me that story?

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:33.801
-That was one of the games
that kind of stood out

00:04:33.801 --> 00:04:35.767
because I was at the brink.

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:41.067
I felt like things
were out of my control.

00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:43.734
I felt like, you know,
I have this amount of talent,

00:04:43.734 --> 00:04:45.067
and I was asked
to take a back seat.

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:46.634
It was very hard
for me to deal with.

00:04:46.634 --> 00:04:48.734
In the middle of the game,
like, Donovan comes up to me,

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:50.033
like, asked me, like,
"Bro, you good?"

00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:51.067
You know, most people tell you,

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:52.567
if I were to ask you,
"Were you good?"

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:56.400
you would tell me you were good
even though you might not be.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.734
Right? So I told him
I was good, and I really wasn't.

00:04:58.734 --> 00:05:01.067
People asked me, like Danny
Ainge asked me if I was okay.

00:05:01.067 --> 00:05:04.067
Told him I was good.

00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:06.767
But in reality, I had, like,
a lot of anxiety, you know,

00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:08.767
dealing with, you know,
that whole scenario last year.

00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:10.834
It was a lot for me. It took
a lot to get out of that.

00:05:10.834 --> 00:05:14.133
-I called Donovan and asked
about that moment, and he said,

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:16.300
you know, "I have known Jaylen
a long time.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.067
I know how he is.
So I knew something was wrong."

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:21.634
-For me, I like
to isolate myself sometimes.

00:05:21.634 --> 00:05:24.767
So not being around people

00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:26.534
was kind of, like, the way
I kind of deal with things,

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:29.267
and it's not always the best way
to kind of deal with stuff.

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:31.067
Family and friends
are necessary.

00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:34.167
Talking about it to people
or having somebody to vent to

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:37.033
or having somebody help lift
you up can be good.

00:05:37.033 --> 00:05:39.167
-You said something that I
thought was interesting

00:05:39.167 --> 00:05:40.868
in that same story
that you're as good

00:05:40.868 --> 00:05:42.267
as the role that you're in.

00:05:42.267 --> 00:05:43.467
What was your role last year,

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:44.534
and what was your role
this year,

00:05:44.534 --> 00:05:46.634
and what do you attribute
that leap to?

00:05:46.634 --> 00:05:48.701
-Space. I think we had a lot
of guys last year.

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:50.434
I think that wasn't a secret.

00:05:50.434 --> 00:05:52.200
Last year, I was asked
to come off the bench

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.667
after coming off
of a great playoffs,

00:05:54.667 --> 00:05:56.767
after coming off
a great second year.

00:05:56.767 --> 00:05:59.667
This year, I'm one of the
starters, one of the leaders,

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:01.534
so I have a lot
more responsibility,

00:06:01.534 --> 00:06:03.167
a lot more weight to carry.

00:06:03.167 --> 00:06:05.934
People had the expectations
of me what I'm doing now,

00:06:05.934 --> 00:06:08.000
but I didn't have
that responsibility.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.667
It's a blessing. I don't look
at it as a negative thing.

00:06:09.667 --> 00:06:11.267
I look at it as a great thing.

00:06:11.267 --> 00:06:12.734
I learned so much more
about myself.

00:06:12.734 --> 00:06:14.701
So I'm not knocking the Celtics.

00:06:14.701 --> 00:06:17.400
It was actually needed for me
to go to where I'm trying to go.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.801
-Jaylen Brown!

00:06:19.801 --> 00:06:21.767
-Got both of you something.

00:06:21.767 --> 00:06:23.767
[ Laughter ]

00:06:26.133 --> 00:06:28.200
-My dawg.
-Got me some lotion.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.968
You know what?
Good looking, though.

00:06:29.968 --> 00:06:31.300
Good looking on the ankles.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.767
Good looking on the ankles.
-Thank you, A.K.

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:37.300
-No problem.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:40.667
-So, what is it like having
Kemba Walker on the team now?

00:06:40.667 --> 00:06:43.200
-Kemba is like somebody, like,

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.567
you knew growing up
all your life,

00:06:45.567 --> 00:06:46.968
just, like, the homey,

00:06:46.968 --> 00:06:49.567
the older brother
that's been through it all,

00:06:49.567 --> 00:06:51.701
seen it all,
and just happy to be here.

00:06:51.701 --> 00:06:53.234
-What's he like as a leader?

00:06:53.234 --> 00:06:56.601
When I saw Kemba's leadership
for the first time was at USA.

00:06:56.601 --> 00:06:59.734
Obviously the goal
that we had didn't plan out

00:06:59.734 --> 00:07:01.801
the way we expected it to.
We all wanted to win.

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:04.334
We didn't want to come home
unless we did win, and we lost.

00:07:04.334 --> 00:07:06.968
Kemba, the way he handled that
and lifted everybody up

00:07:06.968 --> 00:07:08.501
and told us,
"This is not the end.

00:07:08.501 --> 00:07:10.567
Everybody's going to be better
from this," was great for us.

00:07:10.567 --> 00:07:13.234
-Speaking of Team USA,
Jayson Tatum.

00:07:13.234 --> 00:07:14.634
-Mm-hmm.
-First off,

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:18.334
how often do people say "Jayson"
when they mean "Jaylen"

00:07:18.334 --> 00:07:19.934
and "Jaylen"
when they mean "Jayson"

00:07:19.934 --> 00:07:21.868
'cause I always almost do it?

00:07:21.868 --> 00:07:23.234
-Brad does it a lot.

00:07:23.234 --> 00:07:26.534
Brad be drawing up the play
and be saying Jaylen,

00:07:26.534 --> 00:07:29.234
but put "J.T." on the clipboard.

00:07:29.234 --> 00:07:30.734
So me and J.T.
look at each other.

00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:31.901
We're like, "All right, bro.

00:07:31.901 --> 00:07:33.200
One of us is going
to get the bucket."

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.000
Like, It don't even matter.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:39.334
-What is it about Jayson Tatum
that makes him so good?

00:07:39.334 --> 00:07:41.400
That "it" factor,
that niceness factor.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.300
He can do the same move
as somebody else.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.067
It just look better
when he do it.

00:07:45.067 --> 00:07:46.467
You know what I mean?
He just got it.

00:07:46.467 --> 00:07:48.501
Some people got it
and some people don't.

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:51.968
He got that "it" factor,
for sure.

00:07:56.033 --> 00:07:57.734
-Black tee inside
with black jeans?

00:07:57.734 --> 00:07:58.767
What would you like?

00:07:58.767 --> 00:07:59.934
-I'll try the jacket on
right now.

00:07:59.934 --> 00:08:01.467
Growing up in Atlanta,
where I'm from,

00:08:01.467 --> 00:08:03.234
people will talk about you.

00:08:03.234 --> 00:08:04.501
It's a part of the culture.

00:08:04.501 --> 00:08:06.634
You come to school,
and your shoes is busted up,

00:08:06.634 --> 00:08:08.701
people will, like,
make fun of you.

00:08:08.701 --> 00:08:10.234
You know, so growing up
in that culture,

00:08:10.234 --> 00:08:11.300
what you have on your feet

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.400
was always, like,
the most important thing.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.033
To this day, like,
I look at people's shoes

00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:17.067
before I look at their face.
I don't know why that is.

00:08:17.067 --> 00:08:21.667
Just growing up, like, it's just
been conditioned that way.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:23.334
-Who in the NBA style
do you like,

00:08:23.334 --> 00:08:24.901
like, you feel like
they are very well-dressed?

00:08:24.901 --> 00:08:27.000
-P.J. Tucker. I feel like
he's the best-dressed dude

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:28.400
in the league for sure.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.200
Tyson Chandler is the O.G.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.000
If I was 40 years old
and I was 7 foot,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:36.133
I would dress like him.
He got nice style, too.

00:08:36.133 --> 00:08:38.033
-You good?
We got everything you need?

00:08:38.033 --> 00:08:39.634
-Got everything.
-All right, we out?

00:08:39.634 --> 00:08:40.534
-We out.

00:08:40.534 --> 00:08:42.767
-Perfect.
Thank you, A.K.

00:08:42.767 --> 00:08:44.934
Okay, Jaylen,
time to take you home.

00:08:46.934 --> 00:08:51.300
-Okay, true or false and why
is the game, okay?

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.868
You have to pick one.
Don't go between the two.

00:08:53.868 --> 00:08:55.634
You got to pick true or false.
-I got you.

00:08:55.634 --> 00:09:00.534
-Okay. "Queen &amp; Slim" is better
than "Love &amp; Basketball."

00:09:00.534 --> 00:09:02.567
-Whoa.

00:09:02.567 --> 00:09:03.567
I'm going to go false.

00:09:03.567 --> 00:09:04.534
I love "Queen &amp; Slim,"

00:09:04.534 --> 00:09:07.033
but off of G.P.,
general principle,

00:09:07.033 --> 00:09:08.601
I'm going to have to go
"Love &amp; Basketball."

00:09:08.601 --> 00:09:09.901
It's a classic.

00:09:09.901 --> 00:09:14.000
-True or false and why --
during the draft process,

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.367
an exec said
that you were too smart.

00:09:16.367 --> 00:09:19.734
-That was true. I don't know
why the exec thought

00:09:19.734 --> 00:09:22.033
that that would be a bad thing.

00:09:22.033 --> 00:09:26.400
I think when, you know,
athletes are also informative,

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.834
it might intimidate people
for whatever reason.

00:09:28.834 --> 00:09:32.567
LeBron and K.D. with the whole
"Shut Up and Dribble" movement

00:09:32.567 --> 00:09:34.033
was a big push
in the generation

00:09:34.033 --> 00:09:37.467
because athletes get
a pushback from society

00:09:37.467 --> 00:09:40.534
for, you know,
their outside interests,

00:09:40.534 --> 00:09:42.701
and I feel like this generation
that I'm a part of now,

00:09:42.701 --> 00:09:44.734
I think
it's a movement going on,

00:09:44.734 --> 00:09:46.667
and I'm a part
of that movement.

00:09:46.667 --> 00:09:49.167
-Okay. True or false and why --

00:09:49.167 --> 00:09:52.367
I tricked you into saying
you're going to get five rings.

00:09:52.367 --> 00:09:53.667
-True. Very true.

00:09:53.667 --> 00:09:55.968
-I do want to give you
the opportunity to explain,

00:09:55.968 --> 00:09:59.200
'cause I know you feel like
it was taken out of context.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:01.734
-How many championships
28-year-old Jaylen got?

00:10:01.734 --> 00:10:04.267
-I'm not going to jinx myself,
but six years,

00:10:04.267 --> 00:10:06.534
I'm going to go ahead
and say five or six.

00:10:06.534 --> 00:10:08.133
I'm going to say that.

00:10:08.133 --> 00:10:11.000
-So you winning a championship
every year from here to --

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.033
-Yeah, I don't want
to jinx myself.

00:10:12.033 --> 00:10:13.400
-You already did.
You already said it.

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:14.400
-And I don't play to lose.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:15.968
So I'm going to go ahead
and say --

00:10:15.968 --> 00:10:17.601
I'm going to give it a five.

00:10:17.601 --> 00:10:19.667
-I didn't set you up.
I just asked you a question.

00:10:19.667 --> 00:10:20.534
-Right. I didn't say
you set me up.

00:10:20.534 --> 00:10:21.934
-Okay.

00:10:21.934 --> 00:10:24.934
-But the way, you know, they
kind of, like, put it out there,

00:10:24.934 --> 00:10:26.467
the clickbait title was

00:10:26.467 --> 00:10:28.901
"Jaylen Brown said
he's going to have..."

00:10:28.901 --> 00:10:30.868
When people look at that,
like, "Who does Jaylen Brown

00:10:30.868 --> 00:10:33.601
think he is to say he's going to
have five or six rings

00:10:33.601 --> 00:10:35.033
by the time he's 28?"

00:10:35.033 --> 00:10:37.434
You know, I had to answer to,
like, Danny Ainge.

00:10:37.434 --> 00:10:38.801
Like, he came to me
and was like,

00:10:38.801 --> 00:10:39.868
"You're being disrespectful,"

00:10:39.868 --> 00:10:41.767
but I didn't mean
any disrespect,

00:10:41.767 --> 00:10:43.767
'cause I know how hard it is
just to win one.

00:10:43.767 --> 00:10:46.734
I wasn't guaranteeing anything,
I wasn't trying to be arrogant,

00:10:46.734 --> 00:10:48.501
or I wasn't saying
that this is going to happen.

00:10:48.501 --> 00:10:52.200
I was just like, if I wanted
things to go a certain way,

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:53.701
that's the way
I want them to go.

00:10:53.701 --> 00:10:55.634
I'm over it.
I'm past it.

00:10:55.634 --> 00:10:58.934
I'm glad you brought it up,
'cause we low-key had beef.

00:10:58.934 --> 00:11:02.000
-We absolutely had beef.
We absolutely had beef.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.968
We Gucci now, though.

00:11:03.968 --> 00:11:05.133
I was like,
"Don't leave me hanging there.

00:11:05.133 --> 00:11:07.200
We Gucci."

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.834
Take me to your decision
to go to Cal.

00:11:09.834 --> 00:11:12.000
-I was number one, number two,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.601
number three
high school recruit,

00:11:13.601 --> 00:11:16.467
so I could've went to pretty
much any school I wanted to.

00:11:16.467 --> 00:11:18.067
Kentucky, Kansas,
North Carolina,

00:11:18.067 --> 00:11:19.467
UCLA --
all great schools.

00:11:19.467 --> 00:11:21.567
It just wasn't fit for me.

00:11:21.567 --> 00:11:25.400
I was actually sitting next
to a girl in AP World History,

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:28.467
and I was listening to her
narrowing down her list,

00:11:28.467 --> 00:11:30.367
and the reasons why she
was narrowing down her list

00:11:30.367 --> 00:11:32.000
was totally different from mine.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.634
I was looking at jersey colors.

00:11:34.634 --> 00:11:36.934
She was looking at, like,
the business climate

00:11:36.934 --> 00:11:38.000
outside of, like, this.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:39.167
She was looking at all --

00:11:39.167 --> 00:11:41.300
I asked her to help me
narrow down my list.

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:42.634
She looked at some school,
and she was like,

00:11:42.634 --> 00:11:46.634
"No, no, no," and she was like,
"This is a good school,"

00:11:46.634 --> 00:11:48.434
and one of those schools
was Cal.

00:11:48.434 --> 00:11:50.067
-What was her name?
-Michelle.

00:11:50.067 --> 00:11:51.667
-Shout out to Michelle.

00:11:51.667 --> 00:11:52.767
Speaking of that process,

00:11:52.767 --> 00:11:54.467
'cause I heard
a little rumor once,

00:11:54.467 --> 00:11:57.000
how close were you
to going to North Carolina?

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:58.534
-I was very close
to going to UNC.

00:11:58.534 --> 00:12:02.167
To this day, I love,
absolutely adore Coach Roy,

00:12:02.167 --> 00:12:04.167
'cause UNC growing up,
my mom would tell you,

00:12:04.167 --> 00:12:05.834
like, I was the biggest UNC fan.

00:12:05.834 --> 00:12:07.634
I had all the memorabilia.

00:12:07.634 --> 00:12:12.334
I had everything, like, M.J.,
everything, and Roy came.

00:12:12.334 --> 00:12:15.767
When he came on a visit, he
brought, like, Jordan's rings.

00:12:15.767 --> 00:12:17.834
-Oh, he pulled out
all the stops.

00:12:17.834 --> 00:12:19.000
-He did the whole thing.

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:21.400
So it was hard for me not to go.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.534
My mom, she made me
do something interesting.

00:12:23.534 --> 00:12:26.701
When I committed, she made me
call every head coach

00:12:26.701 --> 00:12:28.601
from the schools
that I wasn't going to

00:12:28.601 --> 00:12:30.200
and tell them why
I wasn't going.

00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:31.367
She was like,

00:12:31.367 --> 00:12:32.901
"You're going to have to
conduct business one day,

00:12:32.901 --> 00:12:35.267
and you need to learn how
to manage these relationships,"

00:12:35.267 --> 00:12:38.634
and Roy was one of the very few,
like, one of two, I think,

00:12:38.634 --> 00:12:40.734
that I talked to him
for about an hour,

00:12:40.734 --> 00:12:43.133
and he talked to me about, like,
what he saw in my potential,

00:12:43.133 --> 00:12:44.467
what he compared it to.

00:12:44.467 --> 00:12:48.200
That made me, like,
look at him different.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:50.901
-Absolutely,
'cause genuinely cared.

00:12:50.901 --> 00:12:52.968
-Yeah, so that stuck with me.

00:12:52.968 --> 00:12:54.767
Even though I didn't go to UNC

00:12:54.767 --> 00:12:57.167
and everything turned out
the way it was supposed to,

00:12:57.167 --> 00:13:00.334
I always look back over
my shoulder, like, "What if?"

00:13:00.334 --> 00:13:02.434
We played in preseason
against Charlotte

00:13:02.434 --> 00:13:04.367
two years ago at Chapel Hill.

00:13:04.367 --> 00:13:05.534
I went and gave him a big hug,

00:13:05.534 --> 00:13:07.167
and I almost, like,
dropped a tear.

00:13:07.167 --> 00:13:09.167
To this day, if, Roy, you need
some help with anything,

00:13:09.167 --> 00:13:10.601
recruitment, I got you, bro.

00:13:10.601 --> 00:13:12.601
-He got you.

00:13:12.601 --> 00:13:14.033
Got some good clothes?

00:13:14.033 --> 00:13:16.467
Get your pic off.
[ Laughs ]

00:13:16.467 --> 00:13:18.033
-Here you go.
-Post on the 'Gram.

00:13:18.033 --> 00:13:19.234
You trying to post on the 'Gram?

00:13:19.234 --> 00:13:21.901
-You trying to be funny.
-[ Laughs ] I am funny.

00:13:21.901 --> 00:13:25.300
Well, thank you for letting us
spend a day with you.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:27.267
I took a lot of time
but worth it.

00:13:27.267 --> 00:13:28.567
-No problem. Appreciate you.
My pleasure.

00:13:28.567 --> 00:13:30.367
Thanks so much, Jaylen.
Have a good season.

00:13:30.367 --> 00:13:32.033
-All right. I'm out.

00:13:32.033 --> 00:13:37.634
♪♪

